<section id="simulator">
  <h2 class="kicker">skyBlip Go</h2>

  <%= render "shared/simulator",
        wake: "Hold the lower key to switch it back on.",
        traffic: "Put an aircraft in the sky",
        keys: "Once it is on, the keyboard flies it, and T puts another aircraft in the sky.",
        on: "running",
        off: "off",
        settings: "menu",
        glass: "The simulated e-paper panel of a skyBlip Go",
        key: "The device button: press to open the menu of the page you are on, hold two seconds to switch off, hold it again to switch on",
        pad: "The capacitive pad: tap to change page, hold one second to come back to the radar",
        reset: "RST: reboot the device",
        caption: "Sensors are simulated, the firmware is not. It is the build this site was published from.",
        missing: "The simulator is not part of this build of the site." %>

  <p>
    Nothing is flying out there until you ask for one. Each aircraft the button
    under the case adds is put at a bearing, a range and a height of its own and
    then flies on, broadcasting real ADS-L frames that the firmware receives,
    decodes and plots like any other traffic.
  </p>
  <p>
    The two lamps beside the glass are the device&rsquo;s own. Only the left one
    is the firmware&rsquo;s, lit while a contact is worth calling an alarm. The
    right one is wired to the charger and no line of ours reaches it, so in this
    page, where no cable is ever plugged in, it stays dark.
  </p>
  <p class="note">
    What follows is the device in the order a pilot meets it. The technical
    notes at the foot of the page are the other half: why the screen refreshes
    the way it does, and what the cell is protected from.
  </p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2 class="kicker">The first press</h2>
  <h3>It comes up on the radar and says what it is waiting for.</h3>
  <p class="lede">
    One press of the button on the side and the device is on. There is no boot
    screen to sit through: it goes straight to the traffic picture, and since it
    has no position yet, the ring carries <span class="mono">NO FIX</span> with
    one word under it. <span class="mono">SEARCH</span> is a receiver that is
    solving and has no date yet, <span class="mono">TIME</span> is a date read
    off a satellite, <span class="mono">SILENT</span> is a receiver that has
    stopped answering. That is the difference between a device to wait for and a
    device to move into the open, and it is worth more on the apron than the
    words <span class="mono">NO FIX</span> on their own.
  </p>
  <p>
    The first fix announces itself with a short jingle, and from then on the page is
    a picture of the sky rather than a picture of a search. If a part the device
    cannot fly without did not answer, none of this happens: the self test stays
    on the glass naming the part, the button still works, and the device refuses
    to pretend. Only the radio and the receiver can fail that way. A missing
    buzzer or a board with no barometer reads <span class="mono">NOT FITTED</span>
    and flies.
  </p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2 class="kicker">The picture</h2>
  <h3>Nose up, four miles of sky, and nothing written across the top.</h3>
  <p>
    The plot is heading up. Your own aircraft is drawn nose-up in the middle and
    never turns. The sky turns around it, so a target drawn to the upper right is
    a target to look for off the right of the nose. There is no compass rose and
    nothing at all is written across the top of the glass: the half of the
    picture you are flying into carries the plot and nothing else.
  </p>
  <p>
    Traffic is a circle, and the tag beside it is its height difference in
    hundreds of feet, sitting above the symbol when the aircraft is above you and
    below it when it is below. You know which way to look before you have read
    the digits. A line runs out of each symbol to where that aircraft will be in
    a minute at the speed and track it reported, so a line reaching halfway to
    the ring is an aeroplane crossing two miles in the time it takes to look up.
    A chevron joins the tag when it is climbing or descending at 500&nbsp;ft/min
    or more.
  </p>
  <p>
    The ring is four nautical miles by default and the figure at the foot of the
    page is the label of that circle, in miles or in kilometres depending on what
    you asked for. The glass is wider than the ring, so traffic keeps being
    plotted out into the corners: something is out there, in that direction,
    further than the ring. The count beside the range is the ring only, and the
    clock in the other corner is the time since takeoff, which is the one figure
    on the device a pilot copies into a logbook.
  </p>
  <p>
    What is not a flight is said in the ring, not in a corner:
    <span class="mono">NO FIX</span>, <span class="mono">GROUND</span>,
    <span class="mono">TAXI</span>. A glider pushed to the grid at walking pace
    is taxiing, which is what anybody watching it would say, and the device
    transmits in that state because ADS-L has a ground state and taxiing is it.
  </p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2 class="kicker">When one gets close</h2>
  <h3>One level, and what it tells you is where to look.</h3>
  <p class="lede">
    There is one alarm level. An aircraft inside 3&nbsp;km of you and inside
    300&nbsp;m vertically is a traffic advisory: its circle fills in, the quadrant
    of the glass it is in inverts and flashes once a second, the buzzer says it
    twice, and the lamp beside the screen comes on. The flash is the instruction.
    A quarter of the screen going black and white again is seen without being
    looked at, and a quadrant is the right precision, because the answer is which
    way to turn your head and not where to aim.
  </p>
  <p>
    An advisory brings the radar with it. Reading the instruments or a menu when
    an aircraft enters that window puts you back on the plot, on the pass it
    happens, because the bearing is what you are being told and only that page
    carries it. Walking away again afterwards is yours to do.
  </p>
  <p>
    There is nothing above the advisory and there never will be. The level above
    it in other systems is the resolution advisory, which instructs a crew to
    climb or descend and is coordinated with the other aircraft. This device has
    no link to coordinate over and no authority to give an instruction. The
    loudest thing it can say is look.
  </p>
  <p>
    Once you have the aircraft in sight, hold the pad for a second and the device
    stops talking about it: the tone, the sector and the lamp go together. The
    plot stays exactly as it was, because what you dismissed was a claim about
    what you have seen, not a claim about the sky. It covers the aircraft the
    device has already spoken about and no others, so the next one heard arrives
    with its own voice, and there is no timer on it, because a dismissal that
    expired would shout again about the aeroplane you are looking at.
  </p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2 class="kicker">Walking the device</h2>
  <h3>Three pages, one tap apart. The pad moves, the button acts.</h3>
  <p>
    Two contacts, and the split between them is the whole vocabulary. The
    capacitive pad on the top edge moves you around and can change nothing. The
    button on the side is the one that changes something. Nothing a sleeve
    brushes against can alter a setting, and nothing you do to walk the pages can
    be spent answering a question.
  </p>
  <p>
    A tap of the pad is the next page, and the walk is three: the radar, the list
    of who is out there, the six instrument dials. A touch held for a second is
    the way home to the radar from anywhere. Three and not six, because a pilot
    cycling the glass in flight should pass the plot, the list and the
    instruments, and never the satellite bars.
  </p>

  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table class="modules">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Page</th><th>What it answers</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th>Radar</th><td>where to look: the nose-up plot, the ring, the alarm sector</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Nearby</th><td>who they are: one row per aircraft, nearest first, with its slant range and how far above</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Six-pack</th><td>what you are doing: speed, horizon, altimeter, turn and slip, track, vertical speed, and the flight clock</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <h2 class="kicker">Changing something</h2>
  <h3>The button opens the menu of the page you are standing on.</h3>
  <p class="lede">
    Everything that matters is settable on the device, with no phone. There is no
    single settings screen to go and find: each page has its own menu, and what is
    on it is what that picture is made of. The ring&rsquo;s range and the alarm
    that watches it belong to the radar. The altimeter subscale belongs to the
    page with the altimeter on it. The diagnostic pages hang off the traffic list
    they explain.
  </p>
  <p>
    Inside a menu the two contacts mean what they mean everywhere else: a tap of
    the pad moves the focus down a row, a press of the button acts on the row it
    is on, and every further press steps the same field again, which is how a
    subscale is wound with a thumb. Nothing is a draft. Each press is one finished
    change, so the device cannot be left half edited.
  </p>

  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table class="modules">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>The radar&rsquo;s menu</th><th>What a press does</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th>ID</th><td>nothing. Your ADS-L address, shown and never changed by a thumb</td></tr>
        <tr><th>AIRCRAFT</th><td>next type: glider, microlight, helicopter, paraglider, balloon, and the rest of the ADS-L list</td></tr>
        <tr><th>ALARM</th><td>on or off</td></tr>
        <tr><th>VOLUME</th><td>0 to 5</td></tr>
        <tr><th>RANGE</th><td>the ring: 1, 2, 4 or 8&nbsp;NM, and round again</td></tr>
        <tr><th>UNITS</th><td>nautical, or metric</td></tr>
        <tr><th>STEALTH</th><td>on or off, and it is last because it is the one row that makes this aircraft harder for others to see</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table class="modules">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>The list&rsquo;s menu</th><th>What a press does</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th>RADIO LOG</th><td>opens the tape of every burst heard and sent, newest first</td></tr>
        <tr><th>SATELLITES</th><td>opens one bar per satellite, filled when the receiver used it</td></tr>
        <tr><th>STATUS</th><td>opens what the sensors say, and whether the device is transmitting</td></tr>
        <tr><th>SELF TEST</th><td>opens the power-on self test, one row per part of the board</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table class="modules">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>The instruments&rsquo; menu</th><th>What a press does</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th>G METER</th><td>opens the accelerometer, with the peaks this flight has pulled</td></tr>
        <tr><th>GYRO</th><td>on or off, and off is how a unit ships: the gyroscope sharpens the turn coordinator and the horizon, and it is the one sensor here that costs a milliamp to run</td></tr>
        <tr><th>QNH FROM GNSS</th><td>sets the subscale that makes the altimeter agree with the fix</td></tr>
        <tr><th>QNH - / +</th><td>one hectopascal a press, both signs on one line, because a subscale is wound in both directions</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    Three rules hold in every menu. The rows only ever advance, and the tap past
    the last one hands the glass back to the page the menu belongs to, so the
    thumb that opened a menu closes it with the same gesture. A long touch goes to
    the radar from inside a menu as it does from anywhere else. And a menu nobody
    has touched for a minute gives the picture back on its own. There is no
    <span class="mono">BACK</span> row, because the pad already leaves.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two smaller choices fall out of the same thinking. The unchangeable
    <span class="mono">ID</span> is the first row of the first menu, so a pilot who
    lands there and presses out of impatience presses on nothing. And a page opened
    by name hands you back to the menu that opened it rather than walking on to a
    fourth picture, which is what keeps your thumb where you left it. The range is
    the one value that is not stored: a device always comes up on the 4&nbsp;NM ring
    the radar is designed around.
  </p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2 class="kicker">In your navigation app</h2>
  <h3>It appears by name, and three screens can read it at once.</h3>
  <p>
    The device advertises itself as <span class="mono">skyBlip Go - 5B5AFE</span>:
    the product, then its own 24-bit address, the same one the radar&rsquo;s menu
    shows. Nothing to pair and no code to type. It carries the FLARM NMEA stream
    on both of the Bluetooth serial profiles navigation software looks for, so an
    app built for a Nordic UART device and an app built for a FLARM-style
    <span class="mono">FFE0</span> find the same sentences.
  </p>
  <p>
    Up to three devices can be connected at once and they all read the same
    traffic: the tablet on the yoke, the phone in a pocket, the passenger&rsquo;s
    iPad. Configuration is the one thing that is not shared. The first app to send
    a command keeps that right until it disconnects, and a second one is told who
    holds it rather than left guessing, because two apps writing settings to one
    device is two apps disagreeing about what is stored.
  </p>
  <p>
    Each flight also leaves a record on the device, up to sixteen of them, which
    the same link hands back on the ground.
  </p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2 class="kicker">When the device asks you a question</h2>
  <h3>Two presses allow, one press refuses.</h3>
  <p>
    When an app asks for something that could change the aircraft you are flying,
    new firmware, a settings write, erasing your flights, switching the device
    off, the device does not act on the phone&rsquo;s word. It puts the question
    on its own glass, names it, and waits thirty seconds:
    <span class="mono">PRESS TWICE TO ALLOW</span>,
    <span class="mono">ONE PRESS REFUSES</span>. No answer inside the window is a
    refusal too.
  </p>
  <p>
    Presses only count once the question has actually reached the glass and your
    thumb has stopped, so a run of presses that was stepping a value in a menu
    cannot be spent on an authorisation. The pad is inert while a question stands:
    there are no pages to walk until it is answered. A double press inside
    six tenths of a second is the only one of the button&rsquo;s meanings you have
    to mean to make.
  </p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2 class="kicker">Putting it away</h2>
  <h3>A hold of two seconds switches it off. The same hold with a finger on the pad stores it.</h3>
  <p>
    Switching off is a hold of two seconds. The radio is parked, the panel is given
    three seconds to finish drawing the wordmark, and the rails drop only once your
    thumb is off the button. E-paper keeps its last image with no power at all, so
    the wordmark is what the device wears while it is off: that image on the glass
    means off, not asleep.
  </p>
  <p>
    Putting it away for the season is the same hold with a finger on the pad from
    the start. Same road out, and the glass is left blank instead of wearing the
    wordmark, because one image left on an e-paper for months leaves a shadow the
    panel never fully loses. A device going into a cupboard until the spring should
    be carrying nothing.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two cases are deliberately not what a phone does. A charger plugged into a
    device that is off leaves it off and charging, because a cable found in a
    flight bag is not a request to start transmitting; hold the button while you
    plug in if you want the device as well. And a device that switched itself off
    on a flat cell will not come back on a press: it waits for a charger, so a
    button pressed by the weight of a bag cannot finish the battery off.
  </p>

  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>From off</th><th>What happens</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th>Press the button</th><td>boots, unless the cell is under 3.4&nbsp;V with no charger</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Plug the charger in</th><td>charges, stays off</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Hold the button, plug the charger in</th><td>boots and charges</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Press after a flat-battery shutdown</th><td>nothing, by design: plug the charger in</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Press <span class="mono">RST</span></th><td>boots</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <h2 class="kicker">The third contact</h2>
  <h3><span class="mono">RST</span> reboots, and nothing else does.</h3>
  <p>
    <span class="mono">RST</span> is a hardware reset: press it and the device
    reboots, whatever the firmware was doing. Two presses inside half a second put
    it into the USB bootloader instead, where it appears as a drive you can drop a
    firmware file onto. That route belongs to the bootloader rather than to our
    firmware, which is what makes it the way back from a device that will not
    start.
  </p>
  <p>
    The pad is deliberately the harmless contact of the three. A capacitive pad is
    unusable through winter gloves, and it fires on a cheek and on rain, so nothing
    that matters is behind it: it cannot switch the device on or off, it cannot
    change a setting, and it cannot answer a question on the glass. The worst a pad
    wet with rain can do is turn a page you were not reading, or send a device
    already on its way off with a blank screen instead of a wordmark.
  </p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2 class="kicker">The whole vocabulary</h2>
  <h3>Two contacts, and everything they mean.</h3>

  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Gesture</th><th>On a page</th><th>In a menu</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th>Tap the pad</th>
          <td>the next page</td>
          <td>the next row, and off the last row the page it belongs to</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>Touch the pad, 1&nbsp;s</th>
          <td>silences a standing alarm, and with none standing, the radar</td>
          <td>the radar</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>One press of the button</th>
          <td>opens this page&rsquo;s menu, or refuses a question on the glass</td>
          <td>acts on the focused row, and every further press steps it again</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>Two presses, under 0.6&nbsp;s apart</th>
          <td colspan="2">allows the question on the glass, and means nothing when none stands</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>Hold the button, 2&nbsp;s</th>
          <td colspan="2">switches the device off</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>The same hold, finger on the pad</th>
