        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <h2 class="kicker">Technical notes</h2>
  <h3>The glass</h3>
  <p>
    The panel is a 1.54&Prime; e-paper, 200&times;200 pixels, driven by an SSD1681.
    It holds its last image with the rails down and draws nothing to keep it there,
    which is what makes the parked wordmark a power indicator and what makes a
    blank screen the right thing to store.
  </p>
  <p>
    Every frame in flight is a partial refresh. The full waveform, the one that
    flashes the panel black and white for a second and a half, is used exactly
    twice in the life of a session: once at power on and once at power off. Hours
    of changing frames never cost one. A page change goes through a single black
    wipe of about a third of a second instead, and a second page change made while
    the glass is already black costs no second wipe.
  </p>
  <p>
    A frame identical to the one already on the glass is never presented, and no
    frame is presented more than once a second. That floor is also the alarm&rsquo;s
    blink rate. The part would allow three flips a second and we do not take them:
    a large black region toggled that hard is ghosting the panel never fully loses,
    and one flip a second is already motion in the corner of an eye that should be
    out of the window.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two conditions stop refreshes altogether. Above 50&nbsp;&deg;C read on the
    processor die the panel is left alone, because that is the top of the
    manufacturer&rsquo;s rating for the glass. And at the low-battery cutoff the
    panel is parked as a white field rather than a picture: a cell that low can sag
    under a refresh, and an interrupted waveform is the state that leaves a mark. A
    merely low cell still gets its traffic picture.
  </p>
  <p>
    On the way down the panel&rsquo;s data lines are released before its supply,
    not after. A line left high on a panel with no rail back-feeds it, and the
    parked image drifts dark over the days in a flight bag.
  </p>

  <h3>The cell</h3>
  <p>
    Switching off is a sequence, not a rail drop, and the order is the whole
    content of it. The radio is put to sleep before anything touches its reset
    line. The external flash is told to sleep while it still has a supply to hear
    the command, because after the rail that command is a no-op and the part draws
    its full standby current for the whole night the device spends in a bag looking
    switched off. Then the receiver, then the rails, and the pins are released last.
  </p>
  <p>
    The transmit schedule is the other half of the budget: 1&nbsp;Hz in the air and
    0.1&nbsp;Hz on the ground, as ADS-L asks. The ground second is derived from the
    device&rsquo;s own address, so the parked population spreads itself evenly over
    the ten rather than all speaking at once.
  </p>
  <p>
    The gauge reads two curves, not one. On a charger the terminal sits above the
    cell&rsquo;s own voltage, so the same reading means a far emptier cell, and the
    percentage says which curve it read. It never walks backwards, and a
    22&nbsp;dBm burst sagging the rail does not move it: a gauge that jumps when the
    radio keys is a gauge a pilot stops believing.
  </p>

  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table class="modules">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Threshold</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th>3.50&nbsp;V</th><td>the warning: from here nothing durable is written except the flight record, which is the evidence of the flight that is ending</td></tr>
        <tr><th>3.40&nbsp;V</th><td>a boot on a cell this flat is refused, and the button is left unarmed so a bag cannot keep asking</td></tr>
        <tr><th>3.20&nbsp;V</th><td>the cutoff: the device switches itself off, three consecutive samples and not one, so a transmit sag cannot act</td></tr>
        <tr><th>1.80&nbsp;V</th><td>below this the reading is a floating divider, not a flat cell, and nothing acts on it</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    Nothing acts on a terminal a charger is holding up, and a cell that the
    processor&rsquo;s own power-failure comparator has complained about stops
    durable writes without switching anything off: by then the brownout is
    milliseconds away and there is no orderly shutdown left to run. Charging itself
    is refused outside 5 to 60&nbsp;&deg;C, read on a die that sits above ambient.
  </p>

  <h3>The lamp</h3>
  <p>
    The lamp exists because the screen cannot say whether there is a device behind
    it. Nothing it shows is held solid: every state blinks, and a state held for
    hours is allowed a couple of percent of duty while an alarm may spend thirty.
    Turning the buzzer off does not darken it, because &ldquo;is this thing
    on&rdquo; is not a preference. Charge is not one of its states at all, since
    the charger chip drives its own LED beside it and reports while the processor
    is asleep, which nothing in the firmware can do.
  </p>

  <h3>No pairing, and what stands in for it</h3>
  <p>
    Bluetooth pairing is off on this product, and that is a decision rather than an
    omission. Turning it on encrypts the GATT characteristics, which is a known Web
    Bluetooth failure on Windows, and it would break the browser page that is the
    primary way firmware is installed. So the security boundary is the image and
    not the link. The bootloader verifies a signature over our key and refuses
    anything else, and it refuses an older version than the one installed. An open
    transport cannot install firmware.
  </p>
  <p>
    What an open transport could otherwise do, waste the spare image slot, mark an
    image pending, or reboot the device in flight, is refused by the same rule that
    answers a phone: on the ground only, fail closed, confirmed on the e-paper. No
    pairing, physical presence instead. Every operation that could change the
    aircraft you are flying is authorised by a gesture on the device, which asks
    whoever is asking to be holding it.
  </p>
</section>

